"Shows signed category deviations with bars extending in opposite directions from a meaningful reference.\n\nUse when: Positive and negative changes share a clearly defined zero. The direction and magnitude of departures matter together.\n\nAvoid when: Values cannot be interpreted relative to one shared reference.\n\nVisual grammar: Side of the reference encodes sign. Bar length encodes absolute deviation in a common unit.\n\nBuild: Calculate deviations from the declared target or previous value. Place a prominent zero rule and reserve both sides of the axis. Sort categories by deviation or retain a meaningful ordered sequence.\n\nCheck: Check the subtraction direction is stated explicitly. Confirm symmetric limits when visual balance is part of the comparison. Verify original values and deviations are not mixed.\n\nPitfalls: Color alone should not identify sign when position already can. Percent change is undefined when the comparison denominator is zero.\n\nExample: Show synthetic budget variance by department: negative bars left, positive bars right, with currency labels.\n\nAlternatives: Waterfall chart, Dot plot, Diverging stacked bar chart."
